WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.400
Does the real island follow the rule?

2
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:11.720
The first film used a model island, where wolves usually ran short of food before

3
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.720
the moose ran out.

4
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.260
Now we check the real counts.

5
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:20.593
Isle Royale is an island in Lake Superior, where wolves hunt moose.

6
00:00:22.253 --> 00:00:24.333
Scientists survey both animals in most winters.

7
00:00:25.813 --> 00:00:29.813
Wolves are counted from the air, so the wolf count is close to every wolf.

8
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:33.387
Moose are too many to count one by one.

9
00:00:34.707 --> 00:00:39.367
Scientists count them on small squares of land and use those counts to estimate the

10
00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:42.847
whole island. So moose numbers are estimates.

11
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.850
Here are the counts from many winters.

12
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:48.590
The brown line is the moose.

13
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.030
The grey line is the wolves.

14
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.670
Some winters have no count.

15
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:56.033
Look at nineteen ninety-five.

16
00:00:57.653 --> 00:00:59.553
The moose line is near its highest point.

17
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:02.093
There are only sixteen wolves.

18
00:01:03.293 --> 00:01:06.813
The counter is high, so the wolves had plenty of moose to hunt.

19
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:11.207
Two winters later, the moose had dropped to about five hundred.

20
00:01:12.687 --> 00:01:14.407
The wolves had risen to twenty-four.

21
00:01:15.707 --> 00:01:18.947
The counter fell, and the wolves now had few moose to hunt.

22
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:22.737
The counts alone do not say why the moose fell.

23
00:01:24.177 --> 00:01:25.717
Weather and food can matter too.

24
00:01:26.957 --> 00:01:28.577
What we can follow is the counter.

25
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.880
So here is a question for you.

26
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.920
Only about twenty-one moose for each wolf.

27
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.020
What happens to the twenty-four wolves by the next winter?

28
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.340
Pause and predict.

29
00:01:47.817 --> 00:01:50.777
By the next winter, there were only fourteen wolves.

30
00:01:52.497 --> 00:01:53.797
Hundreds of moose were still alive.

31
00:01:55.017 --> 00:01:56.937
That fits the rule from the first film.

32
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.120
But be careful. One run of counts can fit the rule.

33
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.180
It cannot prove the cause by itself.

34
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:08.053
Now jump to twenty sixteen.

35
00:02:09.613 --> 00:02:12.193
Scientists counted only two wolves on the whole island.

36
00:02:13.393 --> 00:02:16.013
They estimated one thousand three hundred moose.

37
00:02:17.073 --> 00:02:18.513
The counter shoots way up.

38
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:22.293
With so much food, you might expect more wolves.

39
00:02:23.453 --> 00:02:24.453
That did not happen.

40
00:02:25.553 --> 00:02:27.593
The last wolves were close relatives.

41
00:02:29.073 --> 00:02:31.673
Mating between close relatives is called inbreeding.

42
00:02:32.817 --> 00:02:37.657
The wolves carried harmful inherited traits, which added to their health problems.

43
00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:42.097
So a shortage of moose was not what held these wolves back.

44
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.740
Then people stepped in.

45
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.420
In twenty eighteen and twenty nineteen, the Park Service brought nineteen new wolves

46
00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.420
to the island.

47
00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:57.133
So food matters. Few moose for each wolf can mean fewer wolves.

48
00:02:58.273 --> 00:03:03.073
But weather, ticks, disease, inbreeding and people moving wolves also change the

49
00:03:03.073 --> 00:03:07.433
counts. Food is one limit on wolves, not the only one.
